// RT-212 (fixed in 0.30.2: the derived body's path call `Clone::clone(&self.v)` takes Self from its argument as written, `&W` for T = `&W`, RT-159's self_from_written_args; it took the method probe's auto-deref before): (fuller form of R302D-34) `#[derive(Clone)] struct Rep<T> { v: T }` cannot be cloned with T a reference: `Rep { v: &w }.clone()` and `Rep { v: &k }.clone()` are refused in the derive's field (0.30.2)
// rustc prints the EXPECT lines (twin: r302d_034f_derived_clone_generic_struct_of_reference.rs.txt).
// vole: `25:5: error[type error] expected '&W', got 'W' | .. | try: &v: T` and
// `expected '&i64', got 'i64'` at the same field: the derived body clones `self.v`, a
// field place, per instance (R302D-34); the `try: &v: T` it offers would put `&` in
// the struct's field declaration. The derived Clone of `enum E<T> { A(T), B }` with
// `E::A(&w)` is right (a pattern binding). Since v0.28.0.
// EXPECT: Rep { v: W { k: 1 } }
// EXPECT: A(W { k: 1 })
// EXPECT: Rep { v: 5 }
#[derive(Debug)]
struct W {
    k: i64,
}

impl Clone for W {
    fn clone(&self) -> W {
        println("W::clone");
        W { k: self.k + 100 }
    }
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
struct Rep<T> {
    v: T,
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
enum E<T> {
    A(T),
    B,
}

fn main() {
    let w = W { k: 1 };
    let r = Rep { v: &w };
    println("{:?}", r.clone());
    let e = E::A(&w);
    println("{:?}", e.clone());
    let k = 5i64;
    let s = Rep { v: &k };
    println("{:?}", s.clone());
}
